  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}={y}^{3}-7{y}^{2}+15y-9.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y=3$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation\n- $y=-1$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation\n- The equation has $2$ distinct equilibrium solutions",
  "worked_solution": "Given an autonomous differential equation ${y}^{\u2032}(t)=f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is a equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nIn our case, we have that $f(y)={y}^{3}-7{y}^{2}+15y-9.$\n\nLet's examine the statement one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true.  We have that $f(3)=(3)^{3}-7(3)^{2}+15(3)-9=0.$\n\n- Statement II is false.  We have that $f(-1)=(-1)^{3}-7(-1)^{2}+15(-1)-9=-32\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. We know that $y=3$ is a solution to $f(y)=0,$ and so $(y-3)$ is a factor of the polynomial $f(y).$ Dividing $f(y)$ by $(y-3)$ using synthetic division, we get\n\n|  | ${y}^{3}$ | ${y}^{2}$ | ${y}^{1}$ | ${y}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $1$ | $-7$ | $15$ | $-9$ |\n|  | $\u2193$ | $3$ | $-12$ | $9$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-4$ | $3$ | $0$ |\n\n \nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =(y-3)({y}^{2}-4y+3) \\\\  & =(y-3)(y-1)(y-3) \\\\  & =(y-1)(y-3)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(y)$ has $2$ distinct equilibrium solutions, \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(t)=1,\\,{y}_{2}(t)=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
